{"qid":"Q43100","names":["Kashmir"],"title":"Kashmir","endonym":null,"capsule":"Kashmir as a distinct political entity refers to the Hindu Shahi and subsequent dynasties ruling the Vale of Kashmir and adjacent highlands from at least the 6th century CE, with the Karkota dynasty (c. 625–855) establishing the first historically documented kingdom and patronizing Sanskrit scholarship under King Lalitaditya Muktapida (r. c. 724–761), whose armies raided as far as Kanauj and Central Asia. The following Utpala and Lohara dynasties maintained a sophisticated court culture recorded in Kalhana's Rajatarangini (completed 1149), one of the earliest historical chronicles in Sanskrit. Muslim incursions under Shah Mir led to the founding of the Shah Mir Sultanate in 1339, which marked the end of the Hindu dynastic sequence the dataset attests through 1343, though Muslim rulers continued to call the polity Kashmir.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The core Vale of Kashmir is well-defined geographically, but the extent of Kashmiri political authority over neighboring highlands and Lalitaditya's claimed conquests is based on the Rajatarangini, a literary source with known exaggerations; the dataset geometry likely follows the topographically bounded valley rather than claimed imperial extent.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Kashmir","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Kashmir-region-Indian-subcontinent"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kashmir","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kashmir"}],"britannica":"place/Kashmir-region-Indian-subcontinent","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kashmir","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name"